{% set pageTitle = "Import & Export Your Logbook — ForeFlight CSV, PDF, JSON | NinerLog" %}
{% set pageDescription = "Move your flight history into NinerLog from a ForeFlight CSV or any other spreadsheet, and take it out again as CSV, PDF or JSON. Your logbook data is never trapped." %}
{% set pagePath = "/import-export" %}
{% set sitemapChangefreq = "monthly" %}
{% set sitemapPriority = "0.8" %}
{% extends "layouts/base.njk" %}

{% block content %}

<!-- ============================================================
     HERO
     ============================================================ -->
<section class="relative bg-linear-to-br from-navy-900 via-navy-800 to-navy-950 py-16 lg:py-24 overflow-hidden">
  <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-topo-pattern pointer-events-none" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 text-center relative z-10">
    <div class="mb-4 reveal">
      <span class="freq-badge">
        <span class="inline-block w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-cyan-400 animate-blink"></span>
        CSV &bull; FOREFLIGHT &bull; PDF &bull; JSON
      </span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-white text-balance reveal reveal-delay-1">
      Import &amp; Export Your Logbook
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-slate-300 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mx-auto reveal reveal-delay-2">
      Migration pain is the reason NinerLog exists at all. So the way in and the way out both got built properly.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="absolute bottom-0 left-0 right-0 h-px bg-linear-to-r from-transparent via-sky-500/20 to-transparent" aria-hidden="true"></div>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================
     WHY THIS MATTERS
     ============================================================ -->
<section class="bg-white dark:bg-navy-950 py-16 lg:py-24">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4">
    <h2 class="text-2xl lg:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-800 dark:text-white mb-6 reveal">
      Why this page exists.
    </h2>
    <div class="prose prose-slate prose-lg dark:prose-invert max-w-none reveal reveal-delay-1">
      <p>
        NinerLog was started by a pilot who had moved between logbook apps and EFBs more than once, and lost hours each time to cleanup and reformatting. A logbook is a legal record of an entire flying career. It should not be difficult to carry.
      </p>
      <p>
        So import and export are not a checkbox on a feature list here. They are the reason the project got written, and they are the first thing you should test before trusting any logbook with your hours &mdash; including this one.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================
     IMPORT
     ============================================================ -->
<section class="bg-slate-50 dark:bg-navy-900 py-16 lg:py-24">
  <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4">
    <div class="text-center mb-12 lg:mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-3xl lg:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-800 dark:text-white reveal">
        Bringing your history in.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 lg:gap-8">
      <div class="group p-6 bg-white dark:bg-navy-800 border border-slate-200 dark:border-navy-700 rounded-xl hover:shadow-lg hover:-translate-y-1 transition-all duration-200 reveal reveal-delay-1">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-white mb-2">ForeFlight, recognised</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Drop in a ForeFlight logbook CSV and NinerLog identifies the layout from its column headers and maps the fields for you &mdash; times, landings, approaches, holds, instructor names and comments included.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="group p-6 bg-white dark:bg-navy-800 border border-slate-200 dark:border-navy-700 rounded-xl hover:shadow-lg hover:-translate-y-1 transition-all duration-200 reveal reveal-delay-2">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-white mb-2">Any other CSV</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Coming from something else, or from a spreadsheet you have kept by hand? Map each column to a field yourself. Both <code>MM/DD/YYYY</code> and <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code> dates are understood, so US and European exports both work.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="group p-6 bg-white dark:bg-navy-800 border border-slate-200 dark:border-navy-700 rounded-xl hover:shadow-lg hover:-translate-y-1 transition-all duration-200 reveal reveal-delay-3">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-white mb-2">Check before you commit</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          You see the proposed mapping and can correct it before anything is written. Aircraft referenced by your imported flights are created as you go, rather than leaving you to enter a fleet by hand first.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8 text-center text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 max-w-2xl mx-auto reveal reveal-delay-4">
      Once the history is in, currency is calculated from it immediately &mdash; so an imported logbook tells you where you stand under
      <a href="/easa-logbook" class="text-sky-600 dark:text-sky-400 hover:underline">EASA</a> or
      <a href="/faa-logbook" class="text-sky-600 dark:text-sky-400 hover:underline">FAA</a> rules straight away.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================
     EXPORT
     ============================================================ -->
<section class="bg-white dark:bg-navy-950 py-16 lg:py-24">
  <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4">
    <div class="text-center mb-12 lg:mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-3xl lg:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-800 dark:text-white reveal">
        Taking it back out.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 max-w-2xl mx-auto reveal reveal-delay-1">
        No paywall, no record limit, no support ticket. Three formats, because they serve three different purposes.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 lg:gap-8">
      <div class="group p-6 bg-white dark:bg-navy-800 border border-slate-200 dark:border-navy-700 rounded-xl hover:shadow-lg hover:-translate-y-1 transition-all duration-200 reveal reveal-delay-1">
        <div class="font-mono text-xs font-bold text-cyan-500 tracking-wider mb-2">CSV</div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-white mb-2">For moving to another app</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Three column layouts: an <strong>EASA</strong> profile, an <strong>FAA</strong> profile following the familiar ASA/Jeppesen columns, and a <strong>standard</strong> ForeFlight-compatible default. Pick the one your destination expects instead of rearranging columns by hand.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="group p-6 bg-white dark:bg-navy-800 border border-slate-200 dark:border-navy-700 rounded-xl hover:shadow-lg hover:-translate-y-1 transition-all duration-200 reveal reveal-delay-2">
        <div class="font-mono text-xs font-bold text-rose-500 tracking-wider mb-2">PDF</div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-white mb-2">For handing to a human</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          A printable logbook document for an examiner, an employer, a licence application, or a paper file you keep in a drawer because belt and braces is good airmanship.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="group p-6 bg-white dark:bg-navy-800 border border-slate-200 dark:border-navy-700 rounded-xl hover:shadow-lg hover:-translate-y-1 transition-all duration-200 reveal reveal-delay-3">
        <div class="font-mono text-xs font-bold text-emerald-500 tracking-wider mb-2">JSON</div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-white mb-2">For a complete backup</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          The full picture, not just the flight rows &mdash; a structured backup of your logbook data that keeps the relationships between flights, aircraft, licences and ratings intact.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================
     AUTOMATIC BACKUPS
     ============================================================ -->
<section class="bg-slate-50 dark:bg-navy-900 py-16 lg:py-24">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4">
    <h2 class="text-2xl lg:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-800 dark:text-white mb-6 reveal">
      Or have it export itself.
    </h2>
    <div class="prose prose-slate prose-lg dark:prose-invert max-w-none reveal reveal-delay-1">
      <p>
        A manual export only helps if you remember to take one. NinerLog can push scheduled backups to a destination that belongs to you &mdash; any <strong>S3-compatible bucket</strong>, an <strong>SFTP server</strong>, or a <strong>WebDAV share</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your bucket, your credentials, your retention policy. The copy lands somewhere we have no access to, which is rather the point: it keeps working even if this project does not.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================
     HONEST NOTE
     ============================================================ -->
<section class="bg-white dark:bg-navy-950 py-16 lg:py-24">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4">
    <div class="p-6 lg:p-8 bg-amber-500/5 border border-amber-500/30 rounded-xl reveal">
      <h2 class="text-xl lg:text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-800 dark:text-white mb-4">
        What to expect from a migration.
      </h2>
      <div class="prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
        <p class="mb-0">
          Logbook apps disagree about how to model crew, training and remarks, so no import between two systems is ever perfectly lossless &mdash; ours included. Expect to spot-check a sample of entries after importing, particularly around instructor time and approaches. If NinerLog mangles something from your export, that is a bug worth <a href="/report-a-bug">reporting</a>: import fidelity is the part of this project least likely to be left alone.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================
     CTA
     ============================================================ -->
<section class="bg-navy-900 py-16 lg:py-24">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 text-center">
    <h2 class="text-3xl lg:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white mb-6 reveal">
      Bring your hours across.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-lg text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 reveal reveal-delay-1">
      Import your existing logbook, check it, and export it again the same day if you want to. Nothing is locked in.
    </p>
    <a href="https://app.ninerlog.com/register"
       class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-8 py-4 bg-linear-to-r from-amber-400 to-amber-500 text-navy-900 font-semibold text-lg rounded-full shadow-lg shadow-amber-400/30 hover:shadow-xl hover:shadow-amber-400/40 hover:scale-[1.02] active:scale-[0.98] transition-all duration-200 focus-visible:outline-hidden focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-amber-400 focus-visible:ring-offset-2 focus-visible:ring-offset-navy-900 reveal reveal-delay-2">
      Start Logging &mdash; It&rsquo;s Free
    </a>
  </div>
</section>

{% endblock %}
